# Media Support Analysis & Future Implementation

## Why "Video Codec Unsupported" Happens

The error `MEDIA_ERR_SRC_NOT_SUPPORTED` occurs because the application is built on **Electron**, which uses the **Chromium** browser engine to render the UI and play media.

Chromium's built-in HTML5 video player (`<video>`) has strict limitations on what formats it supports out of the box.

### Supported vs. Unsupported Formats

| Feature | Supported by Electron/Chromium | **NOT** Supported (Common in Torrents) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Containers** | MP4, WebM, Ogg | **MKV** (Matroska), AVI, FLV |
| **Video Codecs** | H.264 (AVC), VP8, VP9, AV1 | **H.265 (HEVC)**, VC-1, MPEG-2 |
| **Audio Codecs** | AAC, MP3, Vorbis, Opus, FLAC | **AC3** (Dolby Digital), **E-AC3**, DTS, TrueHD |

**The Issue:** Many high-quality torrent releases use **MKV** containers with **HEVC (H.265)** video and **AC3/DTS** audio. Electron cannot play these natively, leading to the "Video Codec Unsupported" error.

---

## Future Implementation Proposals

To support these formats without forcing the user to use an external player like VLC, we can implement one of the following solutions:

### 1. Real-time Transcoding (FFmpeg)
**Concept:** Use **FFmpeg** (a powerful multimedia framework) in the background to convert unsupported formats into a stream that Chromium can play (e.g., MP4/H.264/AAC) on the fly.

-   **Pros:**
    -   Plays almost *any* file format directly in the app.
    -   No external players required.
-   **Cons:**
    -   High CPU/GPU usage (battery drain on laptops).
    -   Complexity in seeking/scrubbing through the video.
    -   Requires bundling FFmpeg binaries (increases app size).

**Implementation:**
-   Spawn an FFmpeg process in the main Electron process.
-   Pipe the torrent data stream into standard input (stdin) of FFmpeg.
-   FFmpeg outputs a compatible stream to an internal localhost server.
-   The frontend `<video>` tag plays the localhost URL.

### 2. WebAssembly (Wasm) Decoders
**Concept:** Use a JavaScript/Wasm translation of FFmpeg running directly in the browser renderer to decode video frames and paint them to a `<canvas>`.

-   **Pros:**
    -   Portable, runs entirely in the designated view.
-   **Cons:**
    -   Performance is significantly worse than native playback (no hardware acceleration).
    -   Likely to stutter with 1080p+ HEVC content.

### 3. Integrated Native Player (mpv.js)
**Concept:** Instead of using the HTML5 `<video>` tag, embed a native player instance (like **mpv**) directly into the Electron window.

-   **Pros:**
    -   **Best Performance:** Uses native hardware decoding (like VLC).
    -   **Widest Support:** Plays everything mpv/VLC plays (MKV, HEVC, AC3, subtitles).
    -   Feels integrated (not a separate window).
-   **Cons:**
    -   Complex to implement (native node modules).
    -   Harder to overlay HTML UI controls on top of the native surface.

### 4. Custom Electron Build (Not Recommended)
**Concept:** Compile a custom version of Electron with proprietary codecs enabled.
-   **Pros:** Native `<video>` tag support for more formats.
-   **Cons:**
    -   Licensing/Patent issues (H.265/HEVC is patented).
    -   Maintenance nightmare (you have to recompile Electron for every update).
    -   Still doesn't support MKV containers natively.

## Recommendation

**Short Term:**
-   Continue refining the **External Player (VLC)** integration as a fallback for unsupported files.
-   Better detection: Check codec metadata *before* playback and prompt the user to "Play Externally" immediately if unsupported.

**Long Term:**
-   **Solution 1 (Transcoding)** is the most standard approach for media servers (like Plex/Jellyfin) and would work well here if CPU usage is managed.
-   **Solution 3 (mpv integration)** provides the best user experience but requires significant architectural changes.
